ggml: queue user inputs in the backend stream; Vulkan synchronizes without empty submits

The scheduler copied each graph input from the user synchronously: it waited
for the split's backend and then wrote the input with a blocking set_tensor.
On Vulkan without host-visible VRAM, each write was its own queue submission,
fence wait and fence reset, six per decoded token with Qwen3.6 27B.

A new optional backend function, set_tensor_async_staged, queues a write of
host data after the backend's pending work and copies the data before it
returns, so the user may overwrite it at once, which is what the scheduler
needs for inputs. The scheduler uses it when the backend has it and falls
back to the synchronous copy otherwise. Vulkan implements it with host
staging memory owned by the backend context and a copy in the compute stream,
freed once the backend has synchronized.

ggml_vk_synchronize no longer adds an empty queue submission to signal its
fence: the fence (and the wait for transfer-queue work) goes with the last
compute submission, and when only transfer-queue work is outstanding the host
waits for its timeline semaphore.

A host-to-device copy goes on the transfer queue only when that pays off: its
own submission and the compute queue's semaphore wait must cost less than the
copy it lets overlap. The crossover, that overhead times the host-to-device
bandwidth, is measured once per device on first use (the overhead with the
host clock, the bandwidth with GPU timestamps around a staged copy) and
logged; GGML_VK_COMPUTE_STREAM_COPY_MAX sets it instead. Smaller copies go in
the compute stream.

test-backend-sched-inputs checks that inputs are taken when the graph is
queued and ordered against the work already queued on the device, and that
tensors computed on the CPU reach the device on both sides of the copy size
limit (--measured uses the measured limit).

